WEBVTT

00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:14,490
Can you please explain the BU and the bun test on my lab work?

00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:25,010
So the bun on your lab work that's typically seen on a comprehensive metabolic profile. That stands for blood urea, nitrogen.

00:00:25,610 --> 00:00:31,210
And it's a biomarker reflecting how you break down protein and

00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:36,170
how it's how it's how your kidneys aren't getting rid of it.

00:00:36,330 --> 00:00:45,010
So the simplest terminology, if you have an elevated BUN, that means you have stress on your kidneys.

00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:50,210
So your kidneys are undergoing stress for some reason.

00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:56,010
So it's not indicative of, you know, anything, you know,

00:00:57,330 --> 00:00:59,730
again, just like any test, it gives you clues.

00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:04,610
But elevated BUN is their stress out your kidneys.

00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:08,050
Could it be because of kidney dysfunction?

00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:10,450
So the kidneys could be dysfunctioning.

00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,970
So you could have renal disease elevating your BUN.

00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:20,970
You could have something more simple, like even dehydration would elevate your BUN.

00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:27,450
Hypochloridria, beating decreased hydrochloric acid, and your stomach would elevate BUN.

00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:31,370
Because you're going to have incomplete breakdown of proteins in your gut.

00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:37,770
You could have, it's things like access, you know, somebody who's on a

00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:45,170
carnivore type diet where they're eating tons of meat, you know, more than normal.

00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:47,610
They will have an elevated BUN.

00:01:48,610 --> 00:01:49,890
So you kind of got to break it down.

00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:52,250
You're going to look at lifestyle of that person.

00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,450
You wouldn't automatically jump to renal disease.

00:01:55,970 --> 00:02:03,690
But again, so where would we see elevated BUN with somebody who has a previous cancer diagnosis?

00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:08,250
You would have to look at how is this is this person on a medication?

00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:15,130
Because medications can can damage your kidneys and then that can give you an elevated BUN.

00:02:15,130 --> 00:02:19,290
So remember all lab tests are clues to something else.

00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:24,770
So we've seen people on, you know, chemotherapy have an elevated BUN.

00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:30,170
Because the chemotherapy has damaged their kidneys and until that gets cleared out

00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:33,290
and their kidneys can heal, they may have an elevated BUN.

00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:38,970
We've had people with taking venbendazole, or Ivermectin, have an elevated BUNs.

00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:44,690
Similar to them having an elevated alkaline phosphatase, AST, or ALT.

00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:49,090
Those are liver markers showing that that is causing stress to the liver.

00:02:50,210 --> 00:02:53,770
So there's all sorts of things that can cause stress to organs.

00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:59,010
So elevated BUN is going to say, hey, they're stressed to my kidneys.

00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:00,450
What is causing it?

00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:04,450
Is it because of not properly breaking down proteins?

00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,570
Because I have a decreased hydrochloric acid production of my stomach.

00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:09,850
Is it because I have dehydration?

00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:14,370
So I'm having difficulty breaking down proteins and I have elevated urea.

00:03:14,730 --> 00:03:22,930
Do I have kidney damage because of a drug that I'm taking, even a adrenal stress could cause of elevated BUN?

00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,690
And edema could cause elevated BUN.

00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:32,290
So if I have an excess edema due to, let's say, blockage of lymphatic flow could cause an elevated BUN.

00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:37,690
So there's a lot of reasons why that you need to flush them up by looking at the person's history.

00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:43,690
You need to flush out by looking at what else is going on with this person that could be leading to this.

00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:46,690
All blood tests give us clues.

